1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Its golden body and frothy head have enraptured human beings for more than 6,000 years.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Malt, hops, yeast and water.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
But add mashing and boiling, hot and cold, sweat and science.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And the results are lager, pale ale, hefeweizen and porter.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
From microbrew to megabrew.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
From medieval monasteries to a pub near you.

7
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, brewing on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:47,420
If the citizens of the world of beer were to choose a capital,

9
00:00:47,421 --> 00:00:52,320
it might be right here, under the shaded trees

10
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:54,700
of the Augustiner Beer Garden in Munich, Germany.

11
00:00:54,701 --> 00:00:59,700
Here, the beer is still tapped out of wooden barrels,

12
00:00:59,701 --> 00:01:01,700
which are rapidly drained,

13
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,700
until the physically fit staff literally roll out the barrel

14
00:01:11,701 --> 00:01:12,700
again.

15
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:18,700
For the waiters and waitresses who lug up to 12 one-liter glasses

16
00:01:18,701 --> 00:01:21,700
of beer at a time, it's quite a workout.

17
00:01:21,701 --> 00:01:25,700
But for the customers, it's time for the unique form of peace

18
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:29,700
and contentment found only in the beer garden.

19
00:01:29,701 --> 00:01:32,700
The Bavarians call it gemütlich.

20
00:01:32,701 --> 00:01:35,700
I think the word comfortable comes closest.

21
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:37,700
It's a bit more than comfortable.

22
00:01:37,701 --> 00:01:39,700
It's joyful.

23
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:41,700
It's nice, relaxing.

24
00:01:41,701 --> 00:01:42,700
It's more than one meaning.

25
00:01:42,701 --> 00:01:44,700
We say gemütlich.

26
00:01:51,700 --> 00:02:05,700
And it's a wonderful break during the day to sit here and have the best beer in the world.

27
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:17,260
Through the millennia, beer has been and continues to be many things.

28
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:22,000
a drink of celebration, a social lubricant,

29
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:25,060
a tonic at the end of a hard day,

30
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,600
even a valuable source of nutrition.

31
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:30,660
But beer doesn't brew itself.

32
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:38,460
It emerges from a heady mixture of science, sweat, and imagination.

33
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,680
The single greatest source of beer

34
00:02:42,681 --> 00:02:45,740
flows from St. Louis, Missouri-based Anheuser-Busch.

35
00:02:45,741 --> 00:02:50,340
Its breweries account for 12% of all beer on Earth.

36
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,020
You may have heard of their flagship beer.

37
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:58,420
St. Louis Brewery has the capacity to produce about 16

38
00:02:58,421 --> 00:03:00,580
million barrels of beer in a year.

39
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:05,020
31 gallons per barrel, that's the conversion,

40
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:07,120
so you can do the math.

41
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:09,760
It's a large brewery, no question.

42
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:12,420
But despite the size of the place,

43
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:14,700
the brewers here are still just making beer,

44
00:03:14,701 --> 00:03:18,880
turning grains into a carbonated, low-alcohol beverage.

45
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,800
The recipe calls first for malted barley.

46
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:28,980
Malt is the single most important and the largest ingredient in beer.

47
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:35,260
You can malt other grains, but barley is considered to be the superior grain for brewing.

48
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:40,840
Malting the barley consists of soaking the grain in warm water until it begins to germinate,

49
00:03:40,841 --> 00:03:43,580
just enough to activate the enzymes that will convert

50
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:45,980
starch to sugar.

51
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,880
And then just when the green shoot is about to emerge

52
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:50,980
from the barley kernel, you kiln it.

53
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:52,380
You roast it.

54
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,180
Roasting helps determine the flavor

55
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,580
and color of the beer.

56
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:59,600
It also halts the sugar conversion process, which

57
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:02,500
will resume under much more precise conditions

58
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:04,500
in the brew house.

59
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,640
This vessel is called a mash tun,

60
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:09,600
and it's really the beginning of the brewing process.

61
00:04:09,601 --> 00:04:12,600
This is the first time we bring water and grain together.

62
00:04:12,601 --> 00:04:16,600
The purpose of this vessel is to mix the malt and the water

63
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:20,600
and to begin the extraction process of the starches

64
00:04:20,601 --> 00:04:23,600
and the flavors from the malt.

65
00:04:23,601 --> 00:04:27,600
We start at about 109, 110 degrees,

66
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:29,600
and then we slowly raise up the temperature

67
00:04:29,601 --> 00:04:34,600
in order to maximize the enzyme conversion of starch to sugar.

68
00:04:34,601 --> 00:04:36,600
Now, why is that important for a brewer?

69
00:04:36,601 --> 00:04:41,200
brewer because sugar is what the yeast will convert to alcohol

70
00:04:41,201 --> 00:04:44,100
and carbon dioxide during the fermentation.

71
00:04:44,101 --> 00:04:48,640
Brewer's yeast is not able to use starch itself.

72
00:04:48,641 --> 00:04:51,240
It can only feed on sugar.

73
00:04:51,241 --> 00:04:53,760
In the Budweiser recipe, the malted barley

74
00:04:53,761 --> 00:04:57,320
is combined with a second non-malted grain, rice,

75
00:04:57,321 --> 00:05:00,260
which is also used for its sugars and to lighten

76
00:05:00,261 --> 00:05:03,100
the flavor of the beer.

77
00:05:03,101 --> 00:05:05,720
After the barley is offered all its precious sugars,

78
00:05:05,721 --> 00:05:09,220
it makes a final sacrifice.

79
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,820
The husks form a perfect filter bag

80
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:15,260
as the sugary, frothy mixture, now called wort,

81
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,960
is drawn off to the brew kettle.

82
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,400
As you can see, it's a very vigorous boil.

83
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,180
A lot of steam used in this process

84
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:26,280
in order to boil the brew.

85
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:28,840
Boiling goes on for over an hour,

86
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:31,480
and some very important things are happening here.

87
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:34,420
First of all, we're sterilizing the brew with heat.

88
00:05:34,421 --> 00:05:38,220
We're also developing some color during the boiling process.

89
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,860
And we're beginning to precipitate and take some

90
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:43,660
of the protein out of the wort that later on may cause

91
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:47,160
a problem with haze in the beer.

92
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:49,160
It's during the boil that brewers

93
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:53,680
add beer's most characteristic flavor, hops.

94
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:58,080
The pungent, oily, flowering plant known as Humulus lupulus,

95
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:00,780
a relative of cannabis, delivers the smack

96
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:04,420
of bitterness crucial to beer.

97
00:06:04,421 --> 00:06:08,320
A lot of people don't know that hops are actually a flower

98
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:10,640
from a vining perennial plant.

99
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:14,380
And that little flower is densely packed with flavor,

100
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:19,180
both flavor in the form of a very characteristic hop aroma

101
00:06:19,181 --> 00:06:20,840
and also bitterness.

102
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:23,040
Hops are really the spice of beer.

103
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,680
At Anheuser-Busch, the brewmasters taste test

104
00:06:27,681 --> 00:06:30,340
their hop varieties by making a special hop tea.

105
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,120
They also concoct a rice tea.

106
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:39,660
sample the brewery's water, and taste the various stages

107
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:41,860
of wort as it becomes beer.

108
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,860
It's very clean.

109
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,660
Once the boiled wort is filtered,

110
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:49,160
it's ready to leave the hot side of brewing

111
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:54,680
and enter the cold side, where fermentation will begin.

112
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,980
First, the hot wort pipe passes through this heat exchanger,

113
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:02,780
not unlike a giant radiator, where 638 chilled metal plates

114
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:05,980
plates cool the brew quickly.

115
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:08,380
This is crucial to keeping the wort free of contaminating

116
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:13,160
bacteria, which would love to feast on all that sugar.

117
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:17,100
That meal is reserved for one very special guest,

118
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:18,900
brewer's yeast.

119
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:21,660
Well, without yeast, there would be no beer.

120
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:24,940
Yeast is actually a living organism, and they derive their

121
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,300
food energy from the fermentation of sugar.

122
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:30,040
These are single-celled organisms.

123
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:32,760
They consume the sugar, and they convert it into the

124
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:35,920
to alcohol and carbon dioxide.

125
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:38,320
Just as the alcohol gives beer its buzz,

126
00:07:38,321 --> 00:07:40,820
the CO2 provides the fix.

127
00:07:40,821 --> 00:07:43,700
And it all happens here.

128
00:07:43,701 --> 00:07:46,420
This is our primary fermentation cellar.

129
00:07:46,421 --> 00:07:50,000
In here, we have 37 vessels, which contain approximately

130
00:07:50,001 --> 00:07:53,340
180,000 gallons of beer each.

131
00:07:53,341 --> 00:07:55,700
We fill about five of these a day.

132
00:07:55,701 --> 00:07:58,500
It will sit under precise temperature control

133
00:07:58,501 --> 00:08:00,880
and a precise temperature profile.

134
00:08:00,881 --> 00:08:04,380
We use glycol to help cool the fermenter during this time.

135
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:07,060
The glycol is at 30 degrees Fahrenheit,

136
00:08:07,061 --> 00:08:15,460
and we have precise control on that to make sure that the tank stays within plus or minus 0.2 degrees Fahrenheit of its set point.

137
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:20,380
The chilling system counteracts the significant heat produced by fermentation.

138
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:24,440
I have a lot of technology to tell us what's going on here.

139
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:29,940
We have temperature transmitters, yeast growth monitors, and the like.

140
00:08:29,941 --> 00:08:32,340
But what still is more important than that

141
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:35,740
is the overall taste of the beer.

142
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:38,440
A tough job, but brewers around the world

143
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:41,380
are up to the task.

144
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:43,180
At this point in the process, the yeast

145
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:45,680
has doubled, tripled in size.

146
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,000
The yeast will be removed, and the filtration process

147
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:50,200
has removed forward.

148
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:53,600
After the yeast works its magic for five to seven days,

149
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:57,640
the liquid is pumped into one of 378 massive storage tanks

150
00:08:57,641 --> 00:08:59,640
in the brewery's chili cellars.

151
00:08:59,641 --> 00:09:03,640
Each one of these tanks is 120,000 gallons.

152
00:09:03,641 --> 00:09:05,640
The Budweiser, as it's transferred in,

153
00:09:05,641 --> 00:09:07,640
will be stored here for 21 days.

154
00:09:07,641 --> 00:09:09,640
And what's happening during that time frame

155
00:09:09,641 --> 00:09:13,640
is we're developing flavor, maturation,

156
00:09:13,641 --> 00:09:16,640
also the natural carbonation.

157
00:09:16,641 --> 00:09:19,640
After the 21 pressure-filled days in the aging cellar

158
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:22,640
and a final filtration, the golden fermented beverage

159
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:25,640
has earned the name beer.

160
00:09:25,641 --> 00:09:30,600
But the pressure doesn't let up, as the product is pumped into one of three airtight vessels,

161
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:34,620
cans, bottles, and the venerable draft keg.

162
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:42,340
The beer is pumped from our Stockhouse 19, which is our finishing seller, here to a surge tank.

163
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:48,480
And then we deliver the beer through a pump that actually fills the kegs from the bottom up,

164
00:09:48,481 --> 00:09:51,240
ensuring that we purge the keg properly.

165
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,460
This particular line can fill 1,000 kegs per hour.

166
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:59,240
An impressive amount of beer, to be sure, but draft beer makes

167
00:09:59,241 --> 00:10:03,240
up only 10% of Anheuser-Busch's business.

168
00:10:03,241 --> 00:10:06,280
The lion's share of brew is fed into the bottle carousel, which

169
00:10:06,281 --> 00:10:10,680
fills up to 1,300 per minute, and the blindingly fast canning

170
00:10:10,681 --> 00:10:15,400
line, which fills about 2,000 per minute.

171
00:10:15,401 --> 00:10:20,200
That's more than five six-packs every second.

172
00:10:20,201 --> 00:10:23,800
When you pop open one of these, you're lifting the lid on

173
00:10:23,801 --> 00:10:27,800
on thousands of years of brewing history.

174
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:30,540
No one knows when the first beer was brewed.

175
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:33,140
It very well could have been Amazonia,

176
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:36,740
perhaps even New Guinea, where you had complex farming

177
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:41,460
fermentable cereals 10,000 years ago.

178
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:43,080
The earliest physical evidence

179
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:45,160
of a barley-based alcoholic beverage

180
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:49,020
comes from 3500 BC and the Sumerian traders

181
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:51,300
in what's now Iran.

182
00:10:51,301 --> 00:10:55,800
We did an analysis of a very large pottery vessel from this

183
00:10:55,880 --> 00:11:01,940
site, which had inside of it a compound called beer stone or

184
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:06,980
calcium oxalate, which is very specific to barley beer.

185
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:09,320
The inside of the Sumerian brew pot was

186
00:11:09,380 --> 00:11:12,920
grooved, apparently to capture some of this beer stone, an

187
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:15,660
undesirable residue the brewers still must remove from their

188
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:17,800
kettles today.

189
00:11:17,801 --> 00:11:21,200
This small innovation hints at the much larger culture of beer

190
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:23,200
in the ancient Near East.

191
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:26,600
One of the things that's made beer so valuable and so

192
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:29,640
essential to civilization is that it is liquid bread.

193
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:35,020
It was a way to store a crop, ferment it, put alcohol in it,

194
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:38,720
stabilize it, and that people could consume this over a long

195
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:39,880
period of time.

196
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:41,720
There's strong evidence to suggest

197
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:44,300
that it was beer, more so than bread,

198
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,800
that drove these cultures to domesticate cereal crops.

199
00:11:47,801 --> 00:11:49,940
leading to civilization itself.

200
00:11:49,941 --> 00:11:52,280
You know, which came first, bread or beer?

201
00:11:52,281 --> 00:11:55,780
And, you know, if you had your choice, which would it be?

202
00:11:55,781 --> 00:11:58,940
But there's, you know, very practical reasons for why beer

203
00:11:58,941 --> 00:12:02,840
might have been what they were after when they started

204
00:12:02,841 --> 00:12:04,820
domesticating some of these grains.

205
00:12:04,821 --> 00:12:08,120
One would be the nutritional values actually increased when

206
00:12:08,121 --> 00:12:10,260
you go through the fermentation process.

207
00:12:10,261 --> 00:12:14,120
And then you've got the issue of mind-altering effects,

208
00:12:14,121 --> 00:12:17,000
which I don't think should be diminished because, you know,

209
00:12:17,001 --> 00:12:19,040
You know, for early humans especially,

210
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:22,240
this was some sort of a mystery.

211
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:25,340
The fermentation process was also a mystery.

212
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:27,240
But the Sumerians and other early brewers

213
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:29,840
observed that adding honey, dates, or grapes

214
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:32,340
helped get it started.

215
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:35,260
Grapes, dates, figs, and so on can have very high sugar.

216
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:39,660
That attracts insects that are carrying the yeast.

217
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:43,660
But this particular yeast that's so important to beer making,

218
00:12:43,661 --> 00:12:49,000
Sycharomyces cerevisiae is not airborne.

219
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:56,100
It has to be transferred either by an insect or mechanically

220
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:58,300
from one place to another.

221
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,420
While beer today is thought of as the ultimate guy's drink,

222
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,620
ancient brewers were overwhelmingly women.

223
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:07,920
Such was the case in the ancient world's beer capital,

224
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:08,820
Egypt.

225
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:13,420
This is close to 4,000 years old and is from a tomb

226
00:13:13,421 --> 00:13:22,060
What we see here is what goes on to this day in places like Peru and Africa, even Scandinavian countries.

227
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:26,360
We have a woman leaning over a basket.

228
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:31,160
This basket is set on top of a fermenting vessel.

229
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:39,240
It's believed that Egyptian brewers squeezed partially baked bread through the sieve-like basket and mixed it with water in the fermenting vessel.

230
00:13:39,241 --> 00:13:43,740
You had raw dough, which either had a replicated or previously

231
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:47,880
used yeast, or it was spontaneously fermented.

232
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:51,180
Ancient Egyptian society was obsessed with beer.

233
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:53,860
The beverage was produced in massive quantities

234
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:56,420
and served as everything from payment for the workers who

235
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:00,520
built the pyramids to a ritual libation poured into the mouths

236
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:02,500
of the dead.

237
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:05,860
The wealthy were buried alongside entire miniature breweries,

238
00:14:05,940 --> 00:14:08,900
such as this one, ensuring that they were

239
00:14:08,901 --> 00:14:12,200
would have the life-sustaining drink for eternity.

240
00:14:12,300 --> 00:14:17,040
The very symbol for food was a jug of beer and a round cake.

241
00:14:17,140 --> 00:14:21,220
The city of Peluseum on the northeast bank of the Nile

242
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:26,980
Delta was devoted entirely to commercial large-scale brewing

243
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:32,520
of a bewildering variety, 50, 60 varieties of beer, which

244
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:35,760
were shipped as far away as India.

245
00:14:35,761 --> 00:14:40,400
It was, and in my view remains, the world's all-time beer

246
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:44,500
capital, dwarfing even Munich.

247
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:46,240
But as extensive as beer making

248
00:14:46,340 --> 00:14:48,880
was in the ancient world, the art of brewing

249
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:51,280
was just getting started.

250
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:53,840
Royal decrees, monks, and scientists

251
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:56,220
would change the nature of beer forever.

252
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,360
Records from ancient Sumeria contain references

253
00:15:01,460 --> 00:15:05,220
to red beer, white beer, premium beer, beer with a head,

254
00:15:05,221 --> 00:15:06,120
and many others.

255
00:15:12,220 --> 00:15:14,520
The Dogfish Head Brewery in Delaware

256
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:16,160
is a thoroughly modern operation.

257
00:15:19,260 --> 00:15:21,060
But some of its recipes harken back

258
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:23,780
to the very roots of brewing.

259
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:27,980
One of the results is Midas Touch, a beer inspired by a party

260
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:32,880
that took place 2,700 years ago.

261
00:15:32,881 --> 00:15:35,880
Midas Touch is brewed with ingredients based on residue

262
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:39,620
from some of the 157 drinking vessels found buried

263
00:15:39,720 --> 00:15:43,820
alongside the body of an Iron Age Phrygian king.

264
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:46,620
We don't know for sure whether it is really King Midas

265
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:48,320
that's in that tomb.

266
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:51,440
But it's approximately the right time.

267
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:53,140
And we know there was a real King Midas.

268
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:56,040
And it certainly is such a rich tomb that it has to be a member

269
00:15:56,140 --> 00:15:57,840
of the royal family.

270
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:00,680
Using infrared spectrometry and gas

271
00:16:00,681 --> 00:16:03,980
chromatography, anthropologist Patrick McGovern determined

272
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:07,480
that the residues found on these vessels were from a barley-based

273
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:11,320
alcoholic beverage, beer.

274
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:14,120
He worked with Sam Calagione and his team of brewers

275
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:17,840
to resurrect and bottle the ancient brew.

276
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:22,240
Midas Touch contains sugars extracted from malted barley,

277
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:25,340
as well as honey and Moosecat grape juice.

278
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:29,440
It's spiced with saffron.

279
00:16:29,441 --> 00:16:32,540
And it's really like a liquid time capsule, really,

280
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:35,400
that we can step back and say, wow, it's pretty cool to know

281
00:16:35,401 --> 00:16:38,480
this is what our ancestors were experimenting with too

282
00:16:38,481 --> 00:16:39,181
and enjoying.

283
00:16:40,380 --> 00:16:42,600
The number and size of the drinking bowl

284
00:16:42,601 --> 00:16:44,940
suggests that the deceased wasn't the only one

285
00:16:44,941 --> 00:16:46,160
who ended up flat on his back.

286
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:52,260
For those who just wanted to get a slight taste,

287
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:55,140
you have the one liter bowl type here.

288
00:16:55,300 --> 00:16:57,400
But those with a little bit higher status

289
00:16:57,401 --> 00:17:01,600
and greater thirst could go with the two liter variety

290
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:03,540
that I have here.

291
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:05,200
This powerful thirst for beer tended

292
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:08,540
to travel along with the domestication of grains,

293
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:11,540
from the Middle East to Europe, where the art of brewing

294
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:12,440
was reborn.

295
00:17:14,660 --> 00:17:18,160
Through the Middle Ages, beer, or as it was more often called,

296
00:17:18,260 --> 00:17:22,500
ale, was spiced with an herbal mixture called gruet.

297
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:26,160
It ended up being controlled by the church, which licensed

298
00:17:26,161 --> 00:17:29,760
and it contained things like bog myrtle, yarrow,

299
00:17:29,860 --> 00:17:32,600
and various other ingredients that were kept secret

300
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:34,600
by the church.

301
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:37,440
Ale was the lifeblood of the people.

302
00:17:37,540 --> 00:17:39,800
Since the water in ale had been boiled

303
00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:42,280
and the brew sterilized by alcohol,

304
00:17:42,380 --> 00:17:45,440
it was far cleaner than water.

305
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:47,040
It was safe.

306
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:48,680
It tasted good.

307
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:53,480
It cheered one up, one's mood up in the face

308
00:17:53,481 --> 00:17:57,480
death or whatever horrible menace was coming over the hill

309
00:17:57,481 --> 00:17:58,480
next.

310
00:17:58,481 --> 00:18:01,480
Terribly important.

311
00:18:01,481 --> 00:18:04,480
An unbroken tradition of brewing from the Middle Ages

312
00:18:04,481 --> 00:18:09,480
to today can be seen here at the Weinstefan Brewery in Freising,

313
00:18:09,481 --> 00:18:10,480
Germany.

314
00:18:10,481 --> 00:18:15,480
Having brewed beer here since the year 1040, it has earned the

315
00:18:15,481 --> 00:18:20,480
distinction of being the world's oldest working brewery.

316
00:18:20,481 --> 00:18:23,580
This brewery and this region of Bavaria has seen many of the

317
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:27,720
most important changes to beer, including perhaps the earliest

318
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:31,720
use of beer's signature flavor, hops.

319
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:35,220
We know that the using of hops was well known in the 8th

320
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:36,020
century.

321
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:39,340
It was used here also in Weinstefan monastery.

322
00:18:42,440 --> 00:18:45,740
On harvest day, Frank Peiffer and hops farmer Albert

323
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:50,480
Eisenmann inspect their bounty of legendary Hallertau hops.

324
00:18:50,481 --> 00:18:51,181
Wow.

325
00:18:54,280 --> 00:18:58,640
When I open it, you can see the yellow particles inside.

326
00:18:58,641 --> 00:19:00,920
It is a lupulin.

327
00:19:00,921 --> 00:19:03,420
It contains the bitter components of the hops.

328
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:07,640
Very fresh.

329
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:14,740
The introduction of hops ended the age of Gruet

330
00:19:14,741 --> 00:19:17,240
and revolutionized brewing.

331
00:19:17,241 --> 00:19:19,480
Hops are a natural preservative.

332
00:19:19,481 --> 00:19:23,240
They prevent harmful bacteria from spoiling the beer.

333
00:19:23,241 --> 00:19:26,040
With hops, brewers could rely on something other than alcohol

334
00:19:26,041 --> 00:19:28,600
to make their beers last.

335
00:19:28,601 --> 00:19:30,600
Beer could be brewed with less malt,

336
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:32,520
and it could travel farther.

337
00:19:32,521 --> 00:19:34,120
And something else happened.

338
00:19:34,121 --> 00:19:37,380
Medieval beer drinkers grew to love hops bitter taste, which

339
00:19:37,381 --> 00:19:39,820
balanced the sweetness of malt.

340
00:19:39,821 --> 00:19:44,720
In Bavaria by the 16th century, hops were the law of the land.

341
00:19:44,721 --> 00:19:46,780
You have to know that we have a special law

342
00:19:46,781 --> 00:19:50,080
Germany, and also especially in Bavaria.

343
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:55,280
It's called the Reinheitsgebot and was installed in 1516 here

344
00:19:55,380 --> 00:19:58,020
by the Bavarian Dukes.

345
00:19:58,120 --> 00:20:08,140
We only are allowed to use malt, hops, and water.

346
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:11,080
The Reinheitsgebot, or German purity law,

347
00:20:11,180 --> 00:20:14,280
was the first food safety law.

348
00:20:14,281 --> 00:20:18,280
It's still enforced in Bavaria, where the use of unmalted grains,

349
00:20:18,281 --> 00:20:22,280
such as rice or corn, or any added flavors,

350
00:20:22,281 --> 00:20:27,280
are strictly verboten. Centuries later, the law was

351
00:20:27,281 --> 00:20:31,280
amended to include a fourth ingredient, yeast, once it was

352
00:20:31,281 --> 00:20:36,280
understood. Wein Stefan, like many breweries

353
00:20:36,281 --> 00:20:40,280
in Central Europe, began as a monastery.

354
00:20:40,281 --> 00:20:43,280
For centuries, monks were the innovators in the science of

355
00:20:43,281 --> 00:20:44,480
brewing.

356
00:20:44,481 --> 00:20:47,480
The monasteries were the seats of learning,

357
00:20:47,481 --> 00:20:50,320
and so they were furthering the technology of beer and making it

358
00:20:50,321 --> 00:20:51,880
a much more consistent product.

359
00:20:51,881 --> 00:20:55,320
They learned rudimentary heat transfer, how to make the kettles

360
00:20:55,321 --> 00:20:58,420
work better, how to evenly distribute heat, how to control

361
00:20:58,421 --> 00:21:00,940
some of the heat in the mash.

362
00:21:00,941 --> 00:21:06,440
Monasteries produced and drank prodigious amounts of ale.

363
00:21:06,441 --> 00:21:09,440
Despite their mastery, the monks and other medieval beer

364
00:21:09,441 --> 00:21:13,280
makers were still brewing in the dark.

365
00:21:13,281 --> 00:21:16,880
They had yet to learn that yeast was a living organism.

366
00:21:16,980 --> 00:21:19,980
They were only thinking that the ghosts of a brewery

367
00:21:20,080 --> 00:21:21,180
that produced the beer.

368
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:29,900
As early as the 15th century, Bavarian and Bohemian

369
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,740
brewers began cold storing, or in German,

370
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:38,980
lagering their beers in Alpine caves or cellars packed with ice.

371
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:41,980
Over time, they began to notice a change in their beer.

372
00:21:41,981 --> 00:21:45,120
As they put them in a cellar, the beer got a much smoother

373
00:21:45,220 --> 00:21:46,880
taste as time went on.

374
00:21:46,980 --> 00:21:49,880
And actually, what was happening was it was the lager yeast,

375
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:52,220
which can work in cool temperatures.

376
00:21:52,320 --> 00:21:55,520
When they were able to isolate that lager yeast cell and start

377
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:57,220
building up colonies of lager yeast,

378
00:21:57,320 --> 00:21:59,940
they ended up growing a beer that was much smoother and not

379
00:22:00,040 --> 00:22:02,740
as fruity as an ale.

380
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:05,840
And thus, lager, still the most popular beer style

381
00:22:05,940 --> 00:22:07,240
in the world, was born.

382
00:22:07,241 --> 00:22:12,080
Whereas ale yeast is a top-fermenting organism that

383
00:22:12,081 --> 00:22:16,580
works best at warm temperatures, about 70 degrees Fahrenheit,

384
00:22:16,581 --> 00:22:20,180
lager yeast ferments on the bottom of the vessel and thrives

385
00:22:20,181 --> 00:22:23,480
only at cold temperatures, about 40 degrees.

386
00:22:23,481 --> 00:22:28,500
By the mid-19th century, the cleaner-tasting lagers dominated

387
00:22:28,501 --> 00:22:33,000
the European beer style. In the British Isles, though,

388
00:22:33,001 --> 00:22:35,840
In the British Isles, though, strongly flavored ales remained

389
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:39,600
king, and they still are today.

390
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:42,740
Both in England and on the continent, brewing science and

391
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:45,740
technology grew more precise.

392
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:48,840
Thermometers allowed for consistency in temperature, from

393
00:22:48,940 --> 00:22:51,660
mashing all the way to fermentation.

394
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:54,160
And the hydrometer allowed brewers to control the sugar

395
00:22:54,260 --> 00:22:56,660
levels, or gravity.

396
00:22:56,760 --> 00:22:59,960
At the end of the brew day, they're going to measure the

397
00:22:59,961 --> 00:23:05,466
the wort. And you'll take a measurement and it will float in the tube.

398
00:23:05,467 --> 00:23:06,960
And the more sugars

399
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:12,826
that you have in that wort will give you the higher alcohol potential.

400
00:23:12,827 --> 00:23:14,320
And so when we take

401
00:23:14,760 --> 00:23:19,208
that measurement and we've got a lot of sugars, it's going to push this out pretty high.

402
00:23:19,209 --> 00:23:19,909
And

403
00:23:19,910 --> 00:23:24,720
at the end of fermentation, when the sugars are gone and alcohol is present, this thing

404
00:23:24,721 --> 00:23:29,720
begin to fall. In 1842, in the bohemian town

405
00:23:29,721 --> 00:23:37,720
of Pilsen, the new cold fermenting yeast and modern kilns, to more precisely roast the malt,

406
00:23:37,721 --> 00:23:43,720
led to a kind of super lager, a new golden beer known as Pilsner, which would eventually sweep

407
00:23:43,721 --> 00:23:46,720
the world. Pilsner beer, which is in the

408
00:23:46,721 --> 00:23:51,720
lager family, is a result of all that technology that was developed in the 1800s.

409
00:23:51,721 --> 00:23:56,360
The color of this beer comes from very light-colored grains.

410
00:23:56,460 --> 00:23:58,760
And then, again, it was the temperature control

411
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:01,200
that brewers gained in the late 1800s

412
00:24:01,300 --> 00:24:03,160
on an industrial scale.

413
00:24:03,260 --> 00:24:06,840
And it's the father of our American Pilsner-style beers

414
00:24:06,940 --> 00:24:09,300
that so many people enjoy today.

415
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:12,080
American beer, the source of much debate.

416
00:24:12,180 --> 00:24:14,380
But one thing remains clear.

417
00:24:14,480 --> 00:24:17,180
In the 19th century, American brewers

418
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:20,480
would change the industry forever.

419
00:24:20,481 --> 00:24:24,080
At a London brewery on October 16, 1814,

420
00:24:24,180 --> 00:24:27,680
a huge wooden storage vat burst open, flooding the streets

421
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:30,720
with a 25-foot high wave of beer.

422
00:24:30,820 --> 00:24:32,520
Eight people drowned in the flood,

423
00:24:32,620 --> 00:24:36,020
while dozens of others ran to scoop up the free beer.

424
00:24:43,840 --> 00:24:46,780
America's long, often tempestuous love affair

425
00:24:46,781 --> 00:24:50,620
This love affair with beer began in 1620 in the waters

426
00:24:50,621 --> 00:24:53,320
off Plymouth Rock when the passengers on the Mayflower

427
00:24:53,321 --> 00:24:56,020
made a startling discovery.

428
00:24:56,021 --> 00:25:00,220
The Pilgrims didn't mean to come here to Massachusetts.

429
00:25:00,221 --> 00:25:01,620
They knew the weather was crummy.

430
00:25:01,621 --> 00:25:03,220
They wanted to go further south.

431
00:25:03,221 --> 00:25:05,840
But as William Bradford, their diarist, wrote,

432
00:25:05,841 --> 00:25:07,340
we must make our landfall.

433
00:25:07,341 --> 00:25:10,380
Our vittles are much spent, especially our beer.

434
00:25:10,381 --> 00:25:12,840
So the Pilgrims came here to Massachusetts

435
00:25:12,841 --> 00:25:15,340
to stop for a beer.

436
00:25:15,341 --> 00:25:18,180
Since the newcomers couldn't get barley to grow well,

437
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:22,280
the Pilgrims brewed with whatever they could.

438
00:25:22,380 --> 00:25:24,680
They brewed hard cider from apples.

439
00:25:24,780 --> 00:25:27,880
Native Americans taught them to brew with maize.

440
00:25:27,980 --> 00:25:29,380
They didn't have that barley available,

441
00:25:29,480 --> 00:25:32,300
so they were using other fermentables,

442
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:34,700
such as the fructose from the pumpkin

443
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:36,800
or the molasses from cane sugar.

444
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:40,500
And they were using that as the base to ferment.

445
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:44,640
And then instead of hops, they were using spruce.

446
00:25:44,641 --> 00:25:47,680
Barley and hops eventually took hold in the New World,

447
00:25:47,780 --> 00:25:50,780
and American breweries grew along with the colonies.

448
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:52,720
Naturally, the most common beers were heavy,

449
00:25:52,820 --> 00:25:56,560
full-flavored English ales, including a new ale,

450
00:25:56,660 --> 00:26:01,820
characterized by its darkly roasted malt, known as Porter.

451
00:26:01,920 --> 00:26:04,960
It became George Washington's favorite.

452
00:26:05,060 --> 00:26:07,200
Many of the founding fathers were brewers,

453
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:10,840
not just Samuel Adams, but George Washington

454
00:26:10,841 --> 00:26:15,320
left his recipe for porter in the New York Public Library.

455
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:19,420
Thomas Jefferson had a brewery there at Monticello.

456
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:23,440
James Madison had a brewery on his estate

457
00:26:23,441 --> 00:26:26,480
and actually proposed the idea of a national brewery

458
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:28,220
funded by the government.

459
00:26:29,120 --> 00:26:31,400
As conflict with the British became inevitable,

460
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:34,200
beer stood ready to do its patriotic duty.

461
00:26:34,201 --> 00:26:39,200
The taverns were the, as Daniel Webster said,

462
00:26:39,201 --> 00:26:41,200
the headquarters of the revolution.

463
00:26:41,201 --> 00:26:44,200
Take your aim! Fire!

464
00:26:44,201 --> 00:26:47,200
When the Sons of Liberty wanted to have a gathering,

465
00:26:47,201 --> 00:26:49,200
the way they got people to their meetings

466
00:26:49,201 --> 00:26:51,200
was they threw a kegger.

467
00:26:51,201 --> 00:26:53,200
It's still commonly used today

468
00:26:53,201 --> 00:26:55,200
to gather a bunch of people together.

469
00:26:55,201 --> 00:26:58,200
The Sons of Liberty weren't alone.

470
00:26:58,201 --> 00:27:00,200
The militia in various countries

471
00:27:00,201 --> 00:27:03,200
were the people who were in the military.

472
00:27:03,201 --> 00:27:06,440
various towns, they had an established training day.

473
00:27:06,540 --> 00:27:09,140
People weren't coming to the training day.

474
00:27:09,240 --> 00:27:12,640
But when they threw a keg of beer on for the conclusion

475
00:27:12,740 --> 00:27:15,640
of the drills, they started getting a better turnout.

476
00:27:15,740 --> 00:27:17,940
Fire!

477
00:27:18,040 --> 00:27:20,160
The English ales of the revolutionary era

478
00:27:20,260 --> 00:27:23,160
began to yield to a new movement in American brewing

479
00:27:23,260 --> 00:27:28,160
by the 1830s, which saw a large influx of Germans and Czechs

480
00:27:28,260 --> 00:27:31,360
and their state-of-the-art breweries.

481
00:27:31,361 --> 00:27:33,860
These new immigrants brought with them the brewing techniques

482
00:27:33,960 --> 00:27:37,540
of the old country and gradually pushed them further west,

483
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:44,640
Detroit, Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Louis, even Golden, Colorado.

484
00:27:48,740 --> 00:27:51,580
Beginning in the mid-1800s, the new breweries fostered

485
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:54,820
a series of breakthroughs that ushered in the era of modern

486
00:27:54,920 --> 00:27:56,180
brewing.

487
00:27:56,280 --> 00:27:59,020
The first was commercial refrigeration, which

488
00:27:59,021 --> 00:28:03,520
which used liquefied ammonia to keep ice frozen indefinitely.

489
00:28:03,620 --> 00:28:06,920
Suddenly, brewers of lager were free of the Four Seasons.

490
00:28:07,020 --> 00:28:09,860
Artificial refrigeration really enabled brewers

491
00:28:09,960 --> 00:28:15,260
to make beer year round, to not necessarily rely on caves

492
00:28:15,360 --> 00:28:16,880
to do their aging, so they could now

493
00:28:16,980 --> 00:28:19,280
build their aging cellars above ground.

494
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:21,480
Adolphus Busch was the first brewer

495
00:28:21,580 --> 00:28:23,920
to adopt this technology.

496
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:26,120
In the 1870s, he also pioneered use

497
00:28:26,121 --> 00:28:28,820
of the double-walled refrigerated rail car, which

498
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:31,620
drew in outside air and circulated it continuously

499
00:28:31,720 --> 00:28:34,960
past large ice bunkers.

500
00:28:35,060 --> 00:28:38,360
A national system of ice houses kept a steady flow of ice

501
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:40,560
onto the trains.

502
00:28:40,660 --> 00:28:42,880
By the 1880s, barrels of cold beer

503
00:28:42,980 --> 00:28:46,580
could reach from coast to coast, especially with the help

504
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:47,880
of another breakthrough.

505
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:50,880
Pasteurization is the other important innovation that

506
00:28:50,980 --> 00:28:52,920
was introduced into the brewing industry also

507
00:28:53,020 --> 00:28:54,620
by Adolphus Busch.

508
00:28:54,621 --> 00:28:57,520
And pasteurization is a simple process of taking the beer,

509
00:28:57,620 --> 00:29:00,460
gently heating it for a short period of time,

510
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:02,260
cooling it back down.

511
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:07,140
And by doing that, you're able to stop the yeast from growing

512
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:10,440
and allow the beer to maintain some shelf life.

513
00:29:10,540 --> 00:29:13,600
Because prior to that, the beer would leave the brewery

514
00:29:13,700 --> 00:29:16,580
and would usually spoil pretty quickly.

515
00:29:16,680 --> 00:29:19,780
The beer bottle also reached maturity in this era.

516
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:21,720
The renovations in glass technology

517
00:29:21,721 --> 00:29:24,480
that allowed them to have precision control

518
00:29:24,580 --> 00:29:26,520
over the size of bottles.

519
00:29:26,620 --> 00:29:29,460
Before that period, the breweries had to employ people

520
00:29:29,560 --> 00:29:31,260
to bottle by hand.

521
00:29:31,360 --> 00:29:33,360
And the last great innovation, although small,

522
00:29:33,460 --> 00:29:37,260
had a very large effect, was the bottle cap.

523
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:40,080
A simple crimped piece of tin and steel

524
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:42,380
allowed the brewers to cap that bottled beer

525
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:45,180
without having to employ an army of people

526
00:29:45,280 --> 00:29:47,520
installing flip tops on bottles.

527
00:29:47,620 --> 00:29:49,720
Machinery could put this on very easily,

528
00:29:49,721 --> 00:29:52,860
and it allowed the brewers to ship their beer all over the country.

529
00:29:54,060 --> 00:29:57,860
The late 19th century was the golden age of brewing in America.

530
00:29:58,800 --> 00:30:03,420
In 1880, there were 2,300 breweries, large and small.

531
00:30:03,840 --> 00:30:09,820
American brewers were winning gold medals at beer competitions all over the world.

532
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:13,440
American beers were among the most respected in the world,

533
00:30:13,441 --> 00:30:18,140
and American brewers were making rich, flavorful lagers.

534
00:30:18,141 --> 00:30:20,940
The German-American beer barons,

535
00:30:21,040 --> 00:30:23,740
men like Fred Pabst, Joseph Schlitz,

536
00:30:23,840 --> 00:30:26,940
Fred Miller, and Adolphus Busch,

537
00:30:27,040 --> 00:30:30,480
built ever more elaborate saloons and beer gardens,

538
00:30:30,580 --> 00:30:34,080
ushering in an American version of Gemütlichkeit.

539
00:30:34,180 --> 00:30:35,200
The beer of choice?

540
00:30:35,300 --> 00:30:36,400
Lager.

541
00:30:36,500 --> 00:30:38,200
By the end of the 19th century,

542
00:30:38,300 --> 00:30:40,500
ales had almost disappeared.

543
00:30:40,600 --> 00:30:44,940
And lager beer was just immediately complimentary

544
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:46,300
to the American climate.

545
00:30:46,301 --> 00:30:48,940
It was a lighter beer, a smoother beer,

546
00:30:49,040 --> 00:30:52,640
and our hot American summers, people naturally

547
00:30:52,740 --> 00:30:54,840
went for this lighter, smoother taste

548
00:30:54,940 --> 00:30:57,340
that quenched their thirst.

549
00:30:57,440 --> 00:30:59,940
But just as quickly as technology and culture

550
00:31:00,040 --> 00:31:03,060
combined to create the golden age of American brewing,

551
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:06,760
politics and bad fortune conspired to kill it.

552
00:31:06,860 --> 00:31:10,400
World War I brought with it a lot of anti-German sentiment,

553
00:31:10,500 --> 00:31:14,760
and so a lot of American brewers had German roots.

554
00:31:14,761 --> 00:31:16,760
So that made things difficult.

555
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:19,640
Then we had the greatest challenge we've ever faced,

556
00:31:19,740 --> 00:31:23,160
and that is Prohibition, where it became illegal for us

557
00:31:23,260 --> 00:31:25,940
to make beer.

558
00:31:26,040 --> 00:31:29,600
By the time Prohibition was repealed in 1933,

559
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:34,980
the number of American breweries had been reduced to 160.

560
00:31:35,080 --> 00:31:37,480
The Great Depression, the Dust Bowl,

561
00:31:37,580 --> 00:31:40,520
and finally the grain rationing of World War II

562
00:31:40,620 --> 00:31:44,760
drove all but the most tenacious breweries out of business.

563
00:31:44,761 --> 00:31:48,760
And each of these calamities had its effect on the beer itself.

564
00:31:49,760 --> 00:31:52,340
The brewers had less raw materials to work with,

565
00:31:52,780 --> 00:31:56,380
so beer shifted to a slightly different product,

566
00:31:56,480 --> 00:31:59,040
a little bit lighter in body, a little bit lighter in taste.

567
00:31:59,480 --> 00:32:02,520
They started using some things that they knew about from the 1800s,

568
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:05,260
like rice, to lighten the flavor of the beer,

569
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:08,660
and it just shifted the taste of beer and the way America drank.

570
00:32:08,880 --> 00:32:10,620
By the mid-20th century,

571
00:32:10,880 --> 00:32:13,400
the reputation of American beer had taken a hit.

572
00:32:14,760 --> 00:32:17,800
But another revolution was brewing.

573
00:32:17,900 --> 00:32:21,000
Soon, microbrewers and homebrewers

574
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:23,260
would take matters into their own hands.

575
00:32:26,300 --> 00:32:29,100
By the early 20th century, German and Austrian immigrants

576
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:31,480
had also established breweries in Mexico,

577
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:34,140
creating famous lager beers such as Corona,

578
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:37,120
Carta Blanca, Bohemia, and Dos Equis.

579
00:32:41,020 --> 00:32:42,880
In the decades of American affluence

580
00:32:42,881 --> 00:32:46,180
followed the Second World War, the generation who had served

581
00:32:46,181 --> 00:32:49,780
in Europe, and later their baby boom children, returned to the

582
00:32:49,781 --> 00:32:54,020
continent to see the sights and to taste the exotic European

583
00:32:54,021 --> 00:32:57,920
beers. By the 1960s, the import

584
00:32:57,921 --> 00:33:02,140
phenomenon had begun. But not everyone was satisfied

585
00:33:02,141 --> 00:33:05,440
with these European beers, especially by the time they were

586
00:33:05,441 --> 00:33:10,040
unloaded from giant, hot container ships.

587
00:33:10,041 --> 00:33:12,880
The first rumblings of a new generation of American brewers

588
00:33:12,980 --> 00:33:15,780
came out of San Francisco in the 1960s.

589
00:33:15,880 --> 00:33:18,280
Where Fritz Maytag took a failing brewery,

590
00:33:18,380 --> 00:33:22,480
but actually focused on that product that was not popular,

591
00:33:22,580 --> 00:33:25,180
just at the same time that this wave of enthusiasm

592
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:27,300
for different tastes was coming back.

593
00:33:27,400 --> 00:33:29,400
Maytag bought the troubled Anchor Brewery,

594
00:33:29,500 --> 00:33:32,400
which had been in business since 1896.

595
00:33:32,500 --> 00:33:34,700
And eventually, by the time that the 70s rolled out

596
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:38,040
into the 80s, Anchor Brewing was actually profitable again.

597
00:33:38,041 --> 00:33:40,480
So they led the way for a lot of microbreweries.

598
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:46,480
Another success story emerged from an East Coast city

599
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:48,920
whose breweries had nearly ceased to exist.

600
00:33:49,020 --> 00:33:53,920
When I first came to this old brewery here in Boston,

601
00:33:54,020 --> 00:33:55,260
I fell in love with it.

602
00:33:55,360 --> 00:33:57,220
There was just a karma.

603
00:33:57,320 --> 00:34:01,400
And I thought, I want to put my brewery here

604
00:34:01,500 --> 00:34:03,760
in this old brewery, because it reminded me of the breweries

605
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:06,340
that I'd been to when I was a kid.

606
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:11,180
red brick, massive, a little courtyard in the middle.

607
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:14,380
To me, that was what a world-class brewery

608
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:15,380
was going to look like.

609
00:34:17,880 --> 00:34:22,180
The 1980s and 90s saw an explosion of startup breweries,

610
00:34:22,280 --> 00:34:25,700
many of them achieving remarkable success.

611
00:34:25,800 --> 00:34:29,100
These upstart breweries, soon dubbed microbreweries,

612
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:32,700
produced something Americans hadn't tasted in a long time,

613
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:35,200
ale.

614
00:34:35,201 --> 00:34:39,940
One of these rediscovered recipes is India Pale Ale, or IPA,

615
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:44,280
the strong, heavily hopped ale named for the long journey from England to India.

616
00:34:45,480 --> 00:34:49,440
Back when India was a colony of the UK,

617
00:34:50,060 --> 00:34:52,760
and they had all their troops and officers stationed over there,

618
00:34:53,020 --> 00:34:57,300
these guys were dying to have a taste of the old country.

619
00:34:57,640 --> 00:35:00,560
And they'd send the beer to them, and it would get there and be spoiled

620
00:35:00,561 --> 00:35:01,880
because of a long, warm trip.

621
00:35:01,881 --> 00:35:05,980
Through trial and error, they figured out that the stronger they made the beer, the

622
00:35:05,981 --> 00:35:10,340
more hops they stuffed into the barrel, the better the odds that the beer would survive

623
00:35:10,341 --> 00:35:13,760
the trip in good shape.

624
00:35:13,761 --> 00:35:17,720
American brewers have also made prize-winning versions of the thick, quenching beers made

625
00:35:17,721 --> 00:35:22,760
of wheat instead of barley, including the famously cloudy German Hefeweizen.

626
00:35:22,761 --> 00:35:26,000
Hefe means yeast.

627
00:35:26,001 --> 00:35:28,900
So it says it's a wheat beer with the yeast inside,

628
00:35:28,901 --> 00:35:32,900
not filtered with this natural turbidity.

629
00:35:32,901 --> 00:35:33,601
Cheers.

630
00:35:42,040 --> 00:35:44,560
Very good.

631
00:35:44,561 --> 00:35:47,760
And Belgian lambic ales.

632
00:35:47,761 --> 00:35:50,000
In the traditional method of making lambic beer

633
00:35:50,001 --> 00:35:54,400
on a Belgian farm, slats in the fermentation barn open wide,

634
00:35:54,401 --> 00:35:57,400
wild yeasts and even bacteria to carry out the fermentation

635
00:35:57,500 --> 00:35:58,400
process.

636
00:35:58,500 --> 00:36:00,940
Of course, we're not going to rip the top off

637
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:02,340
of our brewery here.

638
00:36:02,440 --> 00:36:05,840
So we basically track down the types of wild yeast and bacteria,

639
00:36:05,940 --> 00:36:08,520
good bacteria, that we're looking for and do our

640
00:36:08,620 --> 00:36:10,080
interpretation of this beer.

641
00:36:10,180 --> 00:36:15,320
One of our slogans, I think, was baby gut bacteria.

642
00:36:15,420 --> 00:36:18,060
One of the ways a smaller brewery differs from a

643
00:36:18,160 --> 00:36:22,020
national brewery is at the back end of production, concentrating

644
00:36:22,021 --> 00:36:24,720
concentrating on filtration instead of the more costly

645
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:26,560
pasteurization.

646
00:36:26,660 --> 00:36:29,360
And then once we get this all hooked up,

647
00:36:29,460 --> 00:36:33,460
we're going to turn the pump on and start pushing the beer

648
00:36:33,560 --> 00:36:38,340
through the paper medium that we have to filter the beer.

649
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:41,340
And this is about 9 microns.

650
00:36:41,440 --> 00:36:45,740
So it's basically a pretty coarse paper filter,

651
00:36:45,840 --> 00:36:51,680
but it's perfect for a brew pub or a small setting like this.

652
00:36:51,681 --> 00:36:58,040
This will take about an hour to push the beer from the fermenter through the filter into the tank here.

653
00:36:58,780 --> 00:37:00,860
We will put it on tap this evening.

654
00:37:02,220 --> 00:37:05,760
Many microbrewers learned how to make beer in homebrewing clubs,

655
00:37:06,060 --> 00:37:08,440
like Southern California's Maltose Falcons.

656
00:37:09,600 --> 00:37:11,520
Named after beer's main fermentable sugar,

657
00:37:12,060 --> 00:37:14,660
they're one of the nation's most respected homebrewers clubs.

658
00:37:15,180 --> 00:37:19,340
You saw folks coming out of various homebrewing organizations, including this club,

659
00:37:19,341 --> 00:37:22,280
who started concerns like Sierra Nevada.

660
00:37:22,900 --> 00:37:25,140
They bring this product forward, and people are going,

661
00:37:25,600 --> 00:37:26,600
my God, this is great.

662
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:30,040
And so now Sierra Nevada is a nationwide concern.

663
00:37:33,320 --> 00:37:36,620
In the semi-controlled chaos of the Falcons' clubhouse,

664
00:37:37,080 --> 00:37:40,780
brewers critique each other's beers from aroma to aftertaste,

665
00:37:41,540 --> 00:37:45,080
while President Drew Beecham maintains order.

666
00:37:46,380 --> 00:37:49,200
For over 30 years, the Falcons have met behind

667
00:37:49,201 --> 00:37:53,100
in Woodland Hills, California, where the home brewer can buy

668
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:57,400
everything from bottle caps and a 60-quart brew kettle

669
00:37:57,500 --> 00:38:03,040
to hops, malted barley, and over 30 types of yeast.

670
00:38:03,140 --> 00:38:04,440
The main difference in technology

671
00:38:04,540 --> 00:38:07,560
is just a matter of scale.

672
00:38:07,660 --> 00:38:09,960
Compare the home brewer's heat transfer coil

673
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:13,360
to this one back at Anheuser-Busch,

674
00:38:13,460 --> 00:38:16,460
or this fermentation tank to this one.

675
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:21,900
Thanks mainly to microbrewers and the home brewing culture

676
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:25,580
from which they sprang, there are now over 1,400 breweries

677
00:38:25,680 --> 00:38:27,740
in the United States.

678
00:38:27,840 --> 00:38:30,340
In 1983, there were only 60.

679
00:38:33,140 --> 00:38:36,960
It looks like another golden age for American brewing.

680
00:38:37,060 --> 00:38:38,980
Meanwhile, those who have made it through the trenches

681
00:38:39,080 --> 00:38:41,080
and sustained a successful brewery

682
00:38:41,180 --> 00:38:42,520
have come to respect the big boys.

683
00:38:45,300 --> 00:38:47,900
I think the big American brewers

684
00:38:47,901 --> 00:38:51,240
are one of the wonders of the beer world.

685
00:38:51,340 --> 00:38:56,640
They are able to produce enormous quantities of beer

686
00:38:56,740 --> 00:38:59,480
at very high quality standards.

687
00:38:59,580 --> 00:39:03,520
And they're making a very light beer.

688
00:39:03,620 --> 00:39:05,660
And if you make a really light beer,

689
00:39:05,760 --> 00:39:08,360
if you have any defects in flavor,

690
00:39:08,460 --> 00:39:11,020
it's immediately perceptible.

691
00:39:11,120 --> 00:39:12,860
The formula must be succeeding,

692
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:17,900
because America's most popular beer is Bud Light.

693
00:39:17,901 --> 00:39:21,240
As beer pours ahead into yet another millennium,

694
00:39:21,340 --> 00:39:24,700
breweries large and small continue to tap new ideas,

695
00:39:24,800 --> 00:39:28,640
including the world's strongest beer.

696
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:32,520
Before October 14, 1978, there had been another kind

697
00:39:32,620 --> 00:39:34,420
of prohibition in America.

698
00:39:34,520 --> 00:39:36,780
On that day, President Jimmy Carter

699
00:39:36,880 --> 00:39:40,320
signed a bill making it legal to brew beer in the home.

700
00:39:44,520 --> 00:39:46,860
In today's world of beer, the glass

701
00:39:46,861 --> 00:39:49,860
is more than half full.

702
00:39:49,960 --> 00:39:53,360
Specialty stores are filled with beers from around the world,

703
00:39:53,460 --> 00:39:58,300
while freshly brewed beer is often just around the corner.

704
00:39:58,400 --> 00:40:00,200
And brewers large and small continue

705
00:40:00,300 --> 00:40:03,520
to explore the boundaries of what beer can be.

706
00:40:03,620 --> 00:40:07,520
Witness the organoleptic hops transducing module.

707
00:40:07,620 --> 00:40:11,620
The way it works is it's a giant food grade cylinder

708
00:40:11,720 --> 00:40:13,620
that's stuffed with whole leaf hops.

709
00:40:13,720 --> 00:40:15,760
You run the beer through the cylinder,

710
00:40:15,761 --> 00:40:18,760
and the alcohol in the beer acts as a solvent

711
00:40:18,860 --> 00:40:21,460
and strips the natural oils off the hop leaves,

712
00:40:21,560 --> 00:40:24,000
puts them right into your pint glass.

713
00:40:24,100 --> 00:40:27,000
And so the hop presence is magnified,

714
00:40:27,100 --> 00:40:30,000
and there's a freshness that you can't get

715
00:40:30,100 --> 00:40:32,000
through any other process.

716
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:34,720
Viva la difference.

717
00:40:34,820 --> 00:40:38,120
But there's more than one way to hop up a beer.

718
00:40:38,220 --> 00:40:40,220
Breweries are targeting late-night clubbers

719
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:43,460
with hybrid energy beers that have a kick of caffeine

720
00:40:43,560 --> 00:40:45,760
or other stimulants.

721
00:40:45,761 --> 00:40:49,000
Samuel Adams has made a huge splash by introducing

722
00:40:49,100 --> 00:40:53,260
the world's strongest beer, weighing in at over 25%

723
00:40:53,340 --> 00:40:56,100
alcohol content, twice that of wine.

724
00:40:56,200 --> 00:40:58,500
This brew has defied the physical limits of what beer

725
00:40:58,600 --> 00:41:01,780
had been for millennia.

726
00:41:01,880 --> 00:41:04,740
The thick amber liquid that emerges has no head.

727
00:41:04,840 --> 00:41:07,120
It tastes more like a spirit than a beer.

728
00:41:07,220 --> 00:41:12,320
Sam Adams Utopias pushes all the brewing processes

729
00:41:12,321 --> 00:41:16,960
processes to their limits to create enormous amounts of

730
00:41:16,961 --> 00:41:23,600
flavor and alcohol in order to take beer into an entire other

731
00:41:23,601 --> 00:41:24,760
realm.

732
00:41:24,761 --> 00:41:27,200
It's just this crazy thing.

733
00:41:27,201 --> 00:41:28,800
It's 51 proof.

734
00:41:28,801 --> 00:41:32,400
It is the strongest naturally fermented beverage in human

735
00:41:32,401 --> 00:41:33,480
history.

736
00:41:33,481 --> 00:41:36,680
Utopias is brewed with massive amounts of malt to create a

737
00:41:36,681 --> 00:41:40,580
highly condensed brew bursting with fermentable sugars.

738
00:41:40,581 --> 00:41:45,220
which we then ferment with this kind of ninja yeast

739
00:41:45,221 --> 00:41:49,700
that we've bred generation after generation

740
00:41:49,701 --> 00:41:55,980
over the last 13 years to take the fermentation to levels

741
00:41:55,981 --> 00:42:00,020
that fermentation's never reached before.

742
00:42:00,021 --> 00:42:03,540
This ninja yeast is bred to be a survivor.

743
00:42:03,541 --> 00:42:06,240
Typically, brewer's yeast dies in an alcohol environment

744
00:42:06,241 --> 00:42:09,020
over 12%.

745
00:42:09,021 --> 00:42:12,060
The brew isn't distilled, but like a spirit,

746
00:42:12,061 --> 00:42:15,360
it's aged for several years in oak barrels.

747
00:42:15,361 --> 00:42:19,560
Because Samuel Adams Utopias is naturally fermented

748
00:42:19,561 --> 00:42:23,440
to reach these alcohol levels and not distilled,

749
00:42:23,441 --> 00:42:29,640
we put into the drink all of the fermentation, esters,

750
00:42:29,641 --> 00:42:31,540
flavors, and aromas.

751
00:42:31,541 --> 00:42:35,720
When you distill, you're taking a very small slice

752
00:42:35,721 --> 00:42:39,220
of those flavors focused really around the ethanol.

753
00:42:39,221 --> 00:42:44,220
So you tend to get the bold, almost ferocious attack

754
00:42:44,780 --> 00:42:47,460
of the ethanol that dominates the flavors.

755
00:42:48,440 --> 00:42:52,420
Every time we do a new release of Utopias,

756
00:42:52,421 --> 00:42:57,420
we are tasting flavors that no brewer has ever created

757
00:42:58,080 --> 00:43:00,680
and no brewer has ever tasted.

758
00:43:00,681 --> 00:43:06,920
At that moment, you almost feel the link with the ancient

759
00:43:07,020 --> 00:43:12,920
Mesopotamians and the medieval monks that were chanting as they

760
00:43:13,020 --> 00:43:14,920
enjoyed their double Bach.

761
00:43:15,020 --> 00:43:19,940
At that moment, we feel this link with 6,000 years of brewing

762
00:43:20,040 --> 00:43:22,340
history.

763
